{#
| Variable          | Type                 | Description |
|-------------------|----------------------|-------------|
| page              | object               |             |
| page.title        | string               |             |
| page.slug         | string               |             |
| orders[]          | Orders[] *Collection |             |
#}
{#
  T-6.01 — the orders list. Technique A: upstream's template is replaced.

  WHY `salla-orders` IS NOT ON THIS PAGE ANY MORE, AND IT IS NOT A PREFERENCE.
  The component renders a **four-column table** — number, total, date, status —
  built with `document.createElement` in `getSingleOrderItem()`. It has no items,
  no address, no totals and no per-order actions, and no stylesheet can add a row
  a component never renders, so technique C was never available. Nor is its data:
  `salla.order.api.fetch` returns `items[]` as `{name, quantity}` with no image
  and no price, so subclassing it (technique B) would have had nothing to draw the
  artboard's product rows with. **Twig is the only route that reaches this design**,
  which is what the backlog entry named.

  THE FILTER IS CLIENT-SIDE, AND THE REASON IS WORTH STATING PLAINLY BECAUSE IT
  HAS A COST. The three options are groups of orders, not statuses: a status
  filter would need status **ids**, which are per-store and unknowable from a
  theme, and no query parameter for this page is documented anywhere — not in the
  SDK types, not in the docs, and no Twig template in this theme or upstream reads
  a query parameter at all. Inventing a parameter name would fail *silently*: the
  page would reload and return the same orders. So the grouping is done here, on
  flags the order actually carries, over the orders already rendered.

  **The cost is that it filters what is loaded, not what exists.** With more
  orders than one page holds, «طلبات بانتظار الدفع» can show fewer than the
  customer has until the rest are loaded. `order-list.js` re-applies the filter
  every time a page arrives, and the count beside the control is what is loaded
  rather than a claim about the whole history. Recorded as OP-10.
#}
{% extends "layouts.customer" %}

{% block inner_content %}
    {% if orders|length %}
        {#
          T-4.17's disclosure, consumed rather than copied. `<details>` carries
          open/closed, `aria-expanded` and the keyboard; `sort-disclosure.js`
          moves the pressed state and closes the panel, and it learned this
          page's parameter name from `data-sort-param` rather than growing a
          second implementation.
        #}
        {#
          The desktop tabs (`Desktop Orders In Progress.svg`, 2026-10-14): from
          1024px the three groups are a row of tabs across the page instead of
          the disclosure below. The same `data-sort-disclosure` contract, so
          sort-disclosure.js applies a press and order-list.js keeps both
          controls showing the same group; each is displayed at its own widths.
        #}
        <div class="order-tabs" role="group" aria-label="{{ trans('theme.orders.filter_label') }}"
             data-sort-disclosure data-sort-param="orders">
            {% for group in ['previous', 'in_progress', 'pending_payment'] %}
                <button type="button"
                        class="order-tabs__tab"
                        data-sort-option="{{ group }}"
                        aria-pressed="{{ group == 'previous' ? 'true' : 'false' }}">{{ trans('theme.orders.filter_' ~ group) }}</button>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>

        <details class="sort-disclosure order-filter" data-sort-disclosure data-sort-param="orders">
            <summary class="sort-disclosure__summary">
                <span class="sr-only">{{ trans('theme.orders.filter_label') }}</span>
                <span class="sort-disclosure__current" data-sort-current>{{ trans('theme.orders.filter_previous') }}</span>
                <i class="sicon-keyboard_arrow_down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </summary>

            <ul class="sort-disclosure__list">
                {#
                  The order is the artboard's: the selected group first, then the
                  other two. `Full Page.pdf` draws exactly these three with a
                  check against «الطلبات السابقة».
                #}
                {% for group in ['previous', 'in_progress', 'pending_payment'] %}
                    <li>
                        <button type="button"
                                class="sort-disclosure__option"
                                data-sort-option="{{ group }}"
                                aria-pressed="{{ group == 'previous' ? 'true' : 'false' }}">
                            <i class="sicon-check-circle sort-disclosure__check" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <span>{{ trans('theme.orders.filter_' ~ group) }}</span>
                        </button>
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </details>

        {% hook 'customer:orders.index.items.start' %}
            {#
              Upstream's own infinite scroll, kept: it is the component that knows
              how to fetch `next_page` and append. `item` names what it animates
              and `container` where it appends, which is why the list element
              carries a class the card does not.
            #}
            <salla-infinite-scroll next-page="{{ orders.next_page }}"
                                   container=".orders-list"
                                   item=".order-card">
                <div class="orders-list" data-testid="store-orders-list" data-orders-list>
                    {% for order in orders %}
                        {% include 'components.orders.card' with { order: order } only %}
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
            </salla-infinite-scroll>
        {% hook 'customer:orders.index.items.end' %}

        {#
          The second empty state, and it is a different sentence from the first.
          «لا توجد طلبات» is wrong when the customer has orders and has filtered
          them out; this one says so and offers the way back. It is hidden until
          the filter empties the list, and it is T-2.14's component either way, so
          the two states cannot drift apart.
        #}
        {#
          T-6.03 — one cancel dialog for the whole list, rendered only where at
          least one order on the page can actually be cancelled. `01 Cancel Order
          Confirmation Pop-up.pdf` draws it over this page, which is why it is
          here and not only on the order's own page.
        #}
        {# A plain loop and flags rather than `|filter` with an arrow function:
           upstream never uses `|filter`, and constructs it never uses are what
           broke rendering on Salla before. The flags are declared outside the
           loop so the loop can set them. #}
        {% set orders_can_cancel = false %}
        {% set orders_can_reorder = false %}
        {% for order in orders %}
            {% if order.can_cancel %}{% set orders_can_cancel = true %}{% endif %}
            {% if order.can_reorder %}{% set orders_can_reorder = true %}{% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% if orders_can_cancel %}
            {% include 'components.orders.cancel-dialog' %}
        {% endif %}

        {# T-6.04 — the same arrangement for reordering. #}
        {% if orders_can_reorder %}
            {% include 'components.orders.reorder-dialog' %}
        {% endif %}

        <div data-orders-empty-filter hidden>
            {% include 'components.ui.empty-state' with {
                icon:         'packed-box',
                message:      trans('theme.orders.none_in_group'),
                action_label: trans('theme.orders.filter_previous'),
                action_attrs: { 'data-orders-reset': '' },
                testid:       'store-orders-filtered-empty'
            } only %}
        </div>
    {% else %}
        {#
          T-2.14's component replaces upstream's bare `.no-content-placeholder`
          column, and adds the thing that column never had: a way onward. B8 ruled
          the empty states are derived from existing components, and this is that
          component with the platform's own sentence in it.
        #}
        {% include 'components.ui.empty-state' with {
            icon:         'packed-box',
            message:      trans('pages.orders.non_orders'),
            action_label: trans('common.elements.back_home'),
            action_href:  link('/'),
            testid:       'store-orders-empty'
        } only %}
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block scripts %}
    <script defer src="{{ 'order.js' | asset }}"></script>
{% endblock %}
